{"qid":"Q739","names":["Republic of Colombia"],"title":"Republic of Colombia","endonym":{"native":"Colombia","lang":"es"},"capsule":"The modern Republic of Colombia emerged from the dissolution of Gran Colombia in 1830–1831; from 1863 to 1886 it operated as the United States of Colombia under a radical federalist constitution, reverting to the centralized Republic of Colombia under the Constitution of 1886. During the twentieth century, Colombia endured the Thousand Days War (1899–1902), the loss of Panama in 1903, and from the 1960s onward a prolonged internal conflict involving FARC guerrillas, paramilitary groups, and drug cartels that killed an estimated 220,000 people over five decades before the 2016 peace accord with the FARC. Colombia remains the world's largest producer of cut flowers and a major petroleum exporter, and its 2016 peace process earned President Juan Manuel Santos the Nobel Peace Prize.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. National boundaries are defined by a series of bilateral treaties; the border with Venezuela (1941 treaty) and Ecuador are well-documented, though maritime delimitation with Nicaragua over the San Andres archipelago was subject to a 2012 ICJ ruling. Internal territorial control in conflict zones (1960s–2016) differed substantially from formal administrative boundaries.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Colombia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Colombia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Colombia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colombia"}],"britannica":"place/Colombia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Colombia","rulers":[{"qid":"Q2736411","name":"José Marí